From: Carlos Alvarado Martinez <45523697+calvarado2004@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 10:50:50 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Hash stored payloads in parallel with overlapped direct reads

A peer that reuses its cached rank re-verifies it on every start with
verify_stored_data(), which read and hashed one payload after another on
one thread through 4 MiB buffered reads. On GB10 that ran at 0.6-0.7 GB/s
while SHA-256 runs at 2.5 GB/s per core and the NVMe gives 9-13 GB/s, so a
280 GB DeepSeek V4.1 TP4 rank took about 7 minutes to start.

- Both payload verify_stored_data() paths hash on the package reader
  verification workers (at most 8, as for package verification).
- Each payload is read in aligned 16 MiB blocks through the payload file
  cached direct-I/O handle on a reader thread, while the previous block is
  hashed. One payload therefore streams at hashing speed, and the reads
  stay out of the page cache, which is GPU memory on unified-memory GPUs.
  A filesystem that refuses direct I/O falls back to buffered reads.

Every payload is still hashed and the first failure in payload order is
returned, so a corrupted warm entry is still rejected and rebuilt.
